\section{Related Work}

This section reviews the papers and tools that directly influenced AMD-Agent. 
For each item, we summarize the methodology or purpose, its strengths and limitations, a short comparison to our work, and how it contributed to our system design. 
Table~\ref{tab:related_work_tools} provides a consolidated overview.

\subsection{pefile}
\textit{pefile} is a Python library for static parsing of Windows Portable Executable (PE) files. 
It exposes PE headers, sections, imports, exports, resources, timestamps, data directories, and other metadata without executing the binary. 

\textbf{Strengths:} Mature and widely used; simple Python API; safe static parsing; exposes many PE structures needed for malware feature extraction.

\textbf{Limitations:} Static-only; cannot observe runtime behavior; packed, corrupted, or intentionally malformed binaries may produce incomplete parsing results.

\textbf{Comparison \& Contribution:} Unlike dynamic analysis tools, \textit{pefile} does not execute malware. This matches our design choice for a lightweight, safe static-analysis pipeline. We use it in the FeatureExtraction stage to extract headers, section statistics, and entropy.

\subsection{EMBER}
Anderson and Roth~\cite{anderson2018ember} introduce EMBER, a well-known dataset and feature representation for static PE malware classification. 

\textbf{Strengths:} Strong malware-specific feature baseline; reproducible; practical for large-scale static classification.

\textbf{Limitations:} Static features can be evaded through packing. The original EMBER feature vector is fixed.

\textbf{Comparison \& Contribution:} EMBER directly inspired our 2304-dimensional EMBER-like static PE feature vector. AMD-Agent applies this philosophy in a live continual-learning pipeline with automatic drift detection.

\subsection{MalwareBazaar API}
MalwareBazaar is a public malware-sharing platform operated by abuse.ch. 

\textbf{Strengths:} Live real-world malware source; metadata-rich; supports SHA256 lookup and sample download.

\textbf{Limitations:} API quotas and availability issues; metadata may be noisy; malware handling requires strict sandboxing.

\textbf{Comparison \& Contribution:} MalwareBazaar is our main malware acquisition source. AMD-Agent uses it in SourceDiscovery and BinaryFetch for PE discovery and sandboxed download.

\subsection{River and ADWIN}
River is an online machine-learning library for streaming data, featuring ADWIN, an adaptive windowing algorithm for detecting changes in data streams.

\textbf{Strengths:} Lightweight; suitable for online settings; provides tested streaming drift-detection algorithms.

\textbf{Limitations:} Drift detection depends heavily on the monitored signal and threshold configuration.

\textbf{Comparison \& Contribution:} River/ADWIN is utilized in our DriftMonitor over section entropy, alongside rolling multivariate feature-shift checks, to trigger our retraining nodes.

\subsection{MADAR}
Rahman et al.~\cite{rahman2025madar} propose MADAR, a continual-learning method for malware analysis based on diversity-aware replay to reduce catastrophic forgetting.

\textbf{Strengths:} Replay helps preserve older knowledge; diversity-aware selection is highly suitable for shifting malware campaigns.

\textbf{Limitations:} Requires stored historical samples; replay alone does not prevent evaluation leakage.

\textbf{Comparison \& Contribution:} MADAR inspired our ModelRetrain component. We use replay samples mixed with newly labeled data to retrain the LightGBM classifier.

\subsection{TESSERACT}
Pendlebury et al.~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} propose TESSERACT, an evaluation methodology for malware classification that reduces temporal bias using chronological splits.

\textbf{Strengths:} More realistic than random splits; explicitly addresses temporal bias.

\textbf{Limitations:} Requires enough labeled samples in each chronological split; metrics become unreliable on single-class splits.

\textbf{Comparison \& Contribution:} TESSERACT inspired our chronological evaluation design, ensuring older data is never evaluated on newer models.

